sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/limb-bone-scaling-vs-mechanical-similarity--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean derivation of the three similarity exponents" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/limb-bone-scaling-vs-mechanical-similarity> ;
    dct:description "The data test needs its targets to be theorems. From the scaling assumptions, prove in Lean, next to the existing strength-to-weight lemmas in ThompsonLaws.Magnitude: geometric similarity (every length proportional to mass to the 1/3) implies cross-sectional area proportional to mass to the 2/3 and circumference to the 1/3; elastic similarity as McMahon stated it (diameter proportional to mass to the 3/8, length to the 1/4) implies area proportional to mass to the 3/4; stress similarity (force proportional to mass and stress equal to force over area held constant) implies area proportional to mass to the 1 and circumference to the 1/2. Prove the three area exponents are distinct. Also prove the posture lemma in elementary form: if effective mechanical advantage scales as mass to the γ, constant bone stress is compatible with area proportional to mass to the 1 − γ. No organism is asserted to follow any of these." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Sorry-free theorems giving area exponents 2/3, 3/4 and 1, circumference exponents 1/3, 3/8 and 1/2, and the posture correction 1 − γ." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: One classical exponent does not follow from the assumptions as formalised. That hypothesis is then withdrawn from the data test rather than forced." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. State the three assumption sets as Lean hypotheses. 2. Prove the area and circumference exponents. 3. Prove the exponents are pairwise distinct. 4. Prove the effective-mechanical-advantage correction 1 − γ. 5. Compile with no sorry and point the data test at these constants." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-03> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-04> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.179.4079.1201> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.2740914> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/limb-bone-scaling-vs-mechanical-similarity> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How do limb bones actually scale with body mass?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.179.4079.1201> dct:title "Size and Shape in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "McMahon 1973, Size and shape in biology" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.2740914> dct:title "Scaling Body Support in Mammals: Limb Posture and Muscle Mechanics" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Biewener 1989, Scaling body support in mammals: limb posture and muscle mechanics" .
}

